[Music]  this is your Tech news briefing for  Monday October 31st I'm Zoe Thomas for  The Wall Street Journal  both virtual and augmented reality are  elements of the metaverse but some  people think AR is the bigger business  opportunity  one company that entered the AR space  pretty early on is Microsoft it unveiled  its Hollow lens headset in 2015 and put  it on sale the next year the device sits  on a user's head like a crown and a  screen over their eyes overlays digital  images onto their view of the real world  but in its efforts to make hololens a  profitable product Microsoft has run up  against a number of stumbling blocks  joining us to discuss what those are and  what it means for Microsoft's prospects  in the metaverse is our Tech reporter  Aaron Tilley hi Aaron thanks for joining  me hey thanks for having me so Aaron can  you just start by explaining what  hololens was originally built for so it  started out as this idea around the  future of gaming consoles and it morphed  as the kind of company shifted and was  placed under  the windows division so it kind of  became both a idea around productivity  as well as gaming it was anything  developers could dream of it as they  brought it out for developers and over  time  um you know consumer adoption they  realized was not going to happen it was  way too expensive and so they kind of  shifted towards the Enterprise selling  to companies and how workers can use  these devices  okay so let's talk a little bit about  the situation now then you know who is  buying hololens there are quite a number  of large corporations using them for  example Mercedes-Benz at U.S service  centers there are technicians wearing  them to help repair uh cars the cars of  their customers I talk to them it's  considerably helped them it's helped  them speed up repair times and then  their single biggest customer here is  the military the US military has this  ambitious plan to  put these smart goggles on soldiers face  so they can be more effective and on the  Battleground and assist with training  scenarios and Microsoft won this  contract that could be worth nearly 22  billion dollars over the next 10 years  all right so how are things going with  that military contract there's been a  lot of back and forth in terms of  usability for soldiers I spoke with the  employees that worked on the project  that said it was very difficult for the  soldiers to use this device it was very  heavy on their head it created a lot of  eye strain they could only wear it for  short periods of time when they're  supposed to be wearing it for you know  hours 12 20 hours out in the field and  it just is not a practical device in  situations that are quite life and death  so there are some challenges to using it  what are things like internally within  the Halloween unit despite some you know  large corporate customers it's very  small volumes very low volumes you know  around 300 000 have been sold since it  came out in 2016. so in terms of a  business for a company the size of  Microsoft this is minuscule so it's  really been a challenge for them to  figure out the use cases and potentially  tap into big markets and also just  advancing the technology is very  challenging each generation maybe you'll  get a wider sort of field of view that  you can see this augmented reality stuff  but it is not a graceful sort of  consumer device it's not getting there  seemingly anytime soon and it's just a  huge huge cost for the company without a  ton of return  I mean given those struggles what is it  like for the people who work in the unit  employees feel quite frustrated for a  number of reasons I've spoken to many  employees current former over the past  year and there's been a lot of feeling  of lack of Direction being overworked  management at times has been troubling  and you know this is a company that was  very early on in this trend and they  have had a considerable amount of talent  that has left for places like apple and  meta trying to do their own smart  goggles what has Microsoft said about  all of this Microsoft they've said a lot  externally you can kind of see them  shift towards this idea that their  approach to what everyone is calling the  metaverse is software defined it is  software based rather than a hardware  based approach which is their hololens  products so they've certainly externally  backed away as far as what they've told  me they say they certainly have not said  that they're dropping out of Hardware  hololens just that their approach to the  metaverse that they call more broadly is  a variety of approaches they they have  Hardware they have software they have  gaming which touches on metaverse as  well so they say they're committed to it  and certainly not backing away so where  does Microsoft go with the hololens now  well they still have Enterprise  customers they're serving here they  still have this U.S military contract  but there was a much more ambitious plan  to address the consumer to try to create  this mass-market product and the company  it needs to focus in this time that you  know everybody's preparing for a  recession so they cut away a lot of that  ambition over the past year I think it  gets to the question of priorities the  company is very focused on its Cloud  business how much are they going to  prioritize a still very speculative  market and one thing about Microsoft in  its history is that they've always been  pretty good at seeing where technology  is going with new types of Hardware but  they never managed to execute on that as  well as a company like apple so I think  speaking with many folks kind of  reflected that reality that this is just  not in their DNA are there lessons then  that other companies that are making a  push into the metaverse should be taking  away from Microsoft's experience for  this to work there needs to be a broad  Market there needs to be a mass market  and how are customers going to be  motivated to put a headset on  that is a huge lift it has to be so good  it has to be better than the iPhone  better than a smartphone and so far no  one's cracked it all right that was our  reporter Aaron Tilley Aaron thanks so  much for joining us yeah thanks for  having me and that's it for today's Tech  news briefing if you want more Tech  stories check out our website wsj.com  I'm Zoe Thomas for The Wall Street  Journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  foreign

